{"tag":"KRX3","kind":"statement","subkind":"theorem","paper":"2610.15360","version":"1","local":"thm-1","file":"","label":"th","number":"1.1","section":"sec-1","order":1,"title":"Theorem","body_md":"\\label{th}\n\t\tFor any two distinct primes $p\\ge3$ and $q\u003e10^{10}$ there exists a periodic group $G$ with two generators $a,b$ and period $pq$ such that:\n\t\t\\begin{enumerate}\n\t\t\t\\item the generator $a$ has order $p$;\n\t\t\t\\item the normal closure $\\langle b\\rangle^G$ of the element $b$ in $G$ is a periodic group of period $q$;\n\t\t\t\\item the inner automorphism $i_{a}$ acts on the subgroup $\\langle b\\rangle^G$ without nontrivial fixed points and is not a splitting automorphism of $\\langle b\\rangle^G$.\n\t\t\\end{enumerate}","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"thm","item":"statement/theorem","head":"environment","counter":"thm","number_from":"render","labels":["th"],"parts":[{"marker":"1.","from":5572,"to":5614},{"marker":"2.","from":5614,"to":5724},{"marker":"3.","from":5724,"to":5898}],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"elsewhere","origin":"own","span":{"file":"Fix-point-free.splitting.tex","from":94,"to":101},"hash":"sha256:bf37e7abecf8689cd7444c55c7af6c59729f8e72f0377b8fe8d689d7871f1e16","minhash":"","census":"2610.15360v1#env-1","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"C5UO","kind":"statement","subkind":"lemma","paper":"2610.15360","version":"1","local":"lem-1","file":"","label":"a,b","number":"3.1","section":"sec-3","order":2,"title":"Lemma","body_md":"\\label{a,b}\n\t\tThe element $a$ has order $p$ in ${F/N}$, and the element $b$ has order $q$ in ${F/N}$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"lem","item":"statement/lemma","head":"environment","counter":"thm","number_from":"render","labels":["a,b"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"here","origin":"own","span":{"file":"Fix-point-free.splitting.tex","from":127,"to":129},"hash":"sha256:f38ebe4ee222faff1302c72fbf16df593681df82d795ed754bb2ebbd57195dee","minhash":"","census":"2610.15360v1#env-2","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"4RH5","kind":"proof","paper":"2610.15360","version":"1","local":"proof-1","file":"","section":"sec-3","order":3,"title":"Proof","body_md":"Since $a$ and $\\gamma(a)$ (see \\eqref{gamma}) both have order $p$ and $\\beta\\circ\\alpha=\\gamma$, the element $\\alpha(a)$ also has order $p$; that is, $a$ has order $p$ in ${F/N}$. Likewise, since $b$ and $\\gamma(b)$ have order $q$, so does $\\alpha(b)$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"proof","item":"proof","head":"environment","scope":"paper","proves":"lem-1","attach":3,"span":{"file":"Fix-point-free.splitting.tex","from":130,"to":132},"hash":"sha256:44fb7f09f45eea3c650842a26a06e63e034cfe56abb6265f8f8e61bdaec77cf4","minhash":"","census":"2610.15360v1#proof-1","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"ZLQY","kind":"statement","subkind":"lemma","paper":"2610.15360","version":"1","local":"lem-2","file":"","label":"pq","number":"3.2","section":"sec-3","order":4,"title":"Lemma","body_md":"\\label{pq}\n\t\tThe group ${F/N}$ is a periodic group of period $pq$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"lem","item":"statement/lemma","head":"environment","counter":"thm","number_from":"render","labels":["pq"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"here","origin":"own","span":{"file":"Fix-point-free.splitting.tex","from":133,"to":135},"hash":"sha256:b7ddfef21495a5a7f5f8640590b96895f611e470ceb5fd51d68eb8c852c512a3","minhash":"","census":"2610.15360v1#env-3","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"4Z51","kind":"proof","paper":"2610.15360","version":"1","local":"proof-2","file":"","section":"sec-3","order":5,"title":"Proof","body_md":"Let $x\\in F$ be arbitrary. Since $F/C=\\langle a\\rangle_p\\times \\langle b\\rangle_q$ is an abelian group of exponent $pq$, we have $\\gamma(x^{pq})=1$, that is, $x^{pq}\\in C$. As $x^{pq}=(x^p)^q$, it follows from \\eqref{N} that $x^{pq}\\in N$. Hence the order of the image of $x$ in $F/N$ divides $pq$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"proof","item":"proof","head":"environment","scope":"paper","proves":"lem-2","attach":3,"span":{"file":"Fix-point-free.splitting.tex","from":136,"to":138},"hash":"sha256:e93fa21d00fd0c2acd2242277002903f436f48a0df43866c6a159a692f728ad0","minhash":"","census":"2610.15360v1#proof-2","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"DGKI","kind":"statement","subkind":"lemma","paper":"2610.15360","version":"1","local":"lem-3","file":"","label":"ab","number":"3.3","section":"sec-3","order":6,"title":"Lemma","body_md":"\\label{ab}\n\t\tThe element $ab$ has order $pq$ in ${F/N}$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"lem","item":"statement/lemma","head":"environment","counter":"thm","number_from":"render","labels":["ab"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"here","origin":"own","span":{"file":"Fix-point-free.splitting.tex","from":139,"to":141},"hash":"sha256:e6503693484ca0db1443d2141e4a93f31439d0b0a268b27b736b06704358bd00","minhash":"","census":"2610.15360v1#env-4","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"W55K","kind":"proof","paper":"2610.15360","version":"1","local":"proof-3","file":"","section":"sec-3","order":7,"title":"Proof","body_md":"By Lemma \\ref{pq}, the order of $ab$ in $F/N$ divides $pq$. We show that $(ab)^{p}\\neq1$ and $(ab)^{q}\\neq1$ in $F/N$. Indeed, if $(ab)^{p}=1$ in $F/N$, then $(ab)^{p}\\in N\\subset C$, and therefore\n\t\t$$1=\\gamma\\big((ab)^{p}\\big)=\\gamma(a)^p\\gamma(b)^p=\\gamma(b)^p$$\n\t\tin the abelian group $F/C=\\langle a\\rangle_p\\times \\langle b\\rangle_q$. This contradicts the fact that $\\gamma(b)$ has order $q\\neq p$.\n\t\t\n\t\tSimilarly, if $(ab)^{q}=1$ in $F/N$, then $(ab)^{q}\\in C$, and hence $1=\\gamma\\big((ab)^{q}\\big)=\\gamma(a)^q\\gamma(b)^q=\\gamma(a)^q$, which contradicts the fact that $\\gamma(a)$ has order $p\\neq q$.\n\t\t\n\t\tSince $p$ and $q$ are distinct primes and the order of $ab$ divides $pq$, it follows that this order equals $pq$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"proof","item":"proof","head":"environment","scope":"paper","proves":"lem-3","attach":3,"span":{"file":"Fix-point-free.splitting.tex","from":142,"to":149},"hash":"sha256:059b7afdc34af3e18f76fc7776a2c9429296686a0c348d04063dbc413fb3f0f2","minhash":"","census":"2610.15360v1#proof-3","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"04TV","kind":"statement","subkind":"lemma","paper":"2610.15360","version":"1","local":"lem-4","file":"","label":"acap","number":"3.4","section":"sec-3","order":8,"title":"Lemma","body_md":"\\label{acap}\n\t\t$\\langle a\\rangle\\cap \\langle b\\rangle^{F/N}=\\{1\\}$, where $\\langle b\\rangle^{F/N}$ is the normal closure of the element $b$ in ${F/N}$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"lem","item":"statement/lemma","head":"environment","counter":"thm","number_from":"render","labels":["acap"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"here","origin":"own","span":{"file":"Fix-point-free.splitting.tex","from":150,"to":152},"hash":"sha256:3db48cb573874d265773947314524f59f4b850e9f7593f0f0604e84a0b8db7a1","minhash":"","census":"2610.15360v1#env-5","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"VA68","kind":"proof","paper":"2610.15360","version":"1","local":"proof-4","file":"","section":"sec-3","order":9,"title":"Proof","body_md":"Consider the sequence of canonical epimorphisms $$\\langle a\\rangle_p\\bigcirc^q \\langle b\\rangle_q \\xrightarrow{\\beta}\\langle a\\rangle_p\\times \\langle b\\rangle_q\\xrightarrow{\\delta}\\langle a\\rangle_p.$$\n\t\tOn the one hand, $\\delta(\\beta(a))=a\\neq1$, so $a\\notin \\mathrm{Ker}(\\delta\\circ\\beta)$, and since $a$ has prime order $p$ in $F/N$ (Lemma \\ref{a,b}), we get $\\langle a\\rangle\\cap \\mathrm{Ker}(\\delta\\circ\\beta)=\\{1\\}$. On the other hand, $\\delta(\\beta(b))=1$, and hence $\\langle b\\rangle^{F/N}\\subset \\mathrm{Ker}(\\delta\\circ\\beta)$. Therefore\n\t\t$$\\langle a\\rangle\\cap \\langle b\\rangle^{F/N}\\subset \\langle a\\rangle\\cap \\mathrm{Ker}(\\delta\\circ\\beta)=\\{1\\}.$$","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"proof","item":"proof","head":"environment","scope":"paper","proves":"lem-4","attach":3,"span":{"file":"Fix-point-free.splitting.tex","from":153,"to":156},"hash":"sha256:876c1ecb7fd84bf6e819c16feb6ecd8216ccba6fa87bb2d550ad36efdd41eff1","minhash":"","census":"2610.15360v1#proof-4","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"EV8V","kind":"statement","subkind":"lemma","paper":"2610.15360","version":"1","local":"lem-5","file":"","label":"bFN","number":"3.5","section":"sec-3","order":10,"title":"Lemma","body_md":"\\label{bFN} The normal closure $\\langle b\\rangle^{F/N}$ of the element $b$ in the group ${F/N}$ is a periodic group of period $q$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"lem","item":"statement/lemma","head":"environment","counter":"thm","number_from":"render","labels":["bFN"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"here","origin":"own","span":{"file":"Fix-point-free.splitting.tex","from":157,"to":158},"hash":"sha256:edaae88af2d35deadbd6ace86f66b24100cfbbd65c33265f70831b27dfe1ae82","minhash":"","census":"2610.15360v1#env-6","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"8D41","kind":"proof","paper":"2610.15360","version":"1","local":"proof-5","file":"","section":"sec-3","order":11,"title":"Proof","body_md":"Let the word $x=a^kb^sc$ of $F$ represent an element of the normal closure $\\langle b\\rangle^{F/N}$, where $0\\le k\u003cp$, $0\\le s\u003cq$ and $c\\in C$. Clearly $b^s, c\\in \\langle b\\rangle^{F/N}$ (the Cartesian subgroup $C$ is generated by the commutators $[a^i,b^j]$, which lie in the normal closure of $b$). Hence the condition $a^kb^sc\\in \\langle b\\rangle^{F/N}$ implies that $a^k\\in \\langle b\\rangle^{F/N}$. By Lemma \\ref{acap}, $a^k=1$ in $F/N$, and since $a$ has order $p$ in $F/N$ (Lemma \\ref{a,b}) and $0\\le k\u003cp$, we obtain $k=0$, so $x=b^sc$. Then $\\gamma(x^q)=\\gamma(b)^{sq}=1$, that is, $x^q\\in C$, and therefore $x^q\\in N$ by \\eqref{N}. Thus the order of $x$ in $F/N$ divides $q$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"proof","item":"proof","head":"environment","scope":"paper","proves":"lem-5","attach":3,"span":{"file":"Fix-point-free.splitting.tex","from":159,"to":161},"hash":"sha256:5a0346d09123f6cf5efd6b59e94a17b49242dd22e924e103da1d114f5b6f6a45","minhash":"","census":"2610.15360v1#proof-5","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"7CFM","kind":"statement","subkind":"lemma","paper":"2610.15360","version":"1","local":"lem-6","file":"","label":"IO","number":"4.1","section":"sec-4","order":12,"title":"Lemma","body_md":"\\label{IO} Let $n\u003e10^{10}$ be odd, and let $G_\\mu$, $\\mu\\in I$, be a family of groups without involutions. In the strictly Burnside $n$-product $\\underset{\\mu\\in I}{\\bigcirc}^nG_\\mu$, the centralizer of any nontrivial element $X$ of $G_\\mu$ lies in $G_\\mu$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"lem","item":"statement/lemma","head":"environment","counter":"thm","number_from":"render","labels":["IO"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"Fix-point-free.splitting.tex","from":165,"to":166},"hash":"sha256:59646e351d0ee216c12a86667a912d2c2d466a3be706937835436c956e6e1195","minhash":"","census":"2610.15360v1#env-7","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"3I57","kind":"statement","subkind":"lemma","paper":"2610.15360","version":"1","local":"lem-7","file":"","label":"1.2","number":"4.2","section":"sec-4","order":13,"title":"Lemma","body_md":"\\label{1.2} In $\\langle a\\rangle_p\\bigcirc^q \\langle b\\rangle_q$, the centralizer of every nontrivial element of $\\langle a\\rangle$ coincides with the cyclic group $\\langle a\\rangle$, and the centralizer of every nontrivial element of $\\langle b\\rangle$ coincides with the cyclic group $\\langle b\\rangle$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"lem","item":"statement/lemma","head":"environment","counter":"thm","number_from":"render","labels":["1.2"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"Fix-point-free.splitting.tex","from":168,"to":169},"hash":"sha256:f8d92f7e06ed67344d805821e651c74067634af5cfbc9d000127619d3fbf6fbd","minhash":"","census":"2610.15360v1#env-8","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"ESL6","kind":"statement","subkind":"lemma","paper":"2610.15360","version":"1","local":"lem-8","file":"","label":"reg","number":"5.1","section":"sec-5","order":14,"title":"Lemma","body_md":"\\label{reg}\n\t\tThe inner automorphism $i_a$ of the group ${F/N}$ is a regular automorphism of order $p$ of the subgroup $\\langle b\\rangle^{F/N}$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"lem","item":"statement/lemma","head":"environment","counter":"thm","number_from":"render","labels":["reg"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"here","origin":"own","span":{"file":"Fix-point-free.splitting.tex","from":171,"to":173},"hash":"sha256:885041fb9122f27f248e4037e1b0bfbb9cc5181e4fee26d47c32bb26c346d3b2","minhash":"","census":"2610.15360v1#env-9","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"RFZN","kind":"proof","paper":"2610.15360","version":"1","local":"proof-6","file":"","section":"sec-5","order":15,"title":"Proof","body_md":"By Lemma \\ref{1.2}, the centralizer of $a$ in $F/N=\\langle a\\rangle_p\\bigcirc^q \\langle b\\rangle_q$ is the cyclic group $\\langle a\\rangle$. Since $\\langle a\\rangle\\cap\\langle b\\rangle^{F/N}=\\{1\\}$ by Lemma \\ref{acap}, we have $i_a(x)=a^{-1}xa\\neq x$ for every nontrivial $x\\in \\langle b\\rangle^{F/N}$.\n\t\t\n\t\tIt remains to determine the order of the restriction of $i_a$ to $\\langle b\\rangle^{F/N}$. Since $a^p=1$, we have $i_a^p=i_{a^p}=1$. Suppose that $i_a^k=i_{a^k}$ acts trivially on $\\langle b\\rangle^{F/N}$ for some $0\u003ck\u003cp$. Then $a^k$ centralizes $b$, so $a^k\\in\\langle b\\rangle$ by Lemma \\ref{1.2}. Hence $a^k\\in\\langle a\\rangle\\cap\\langle b\\rangle^{F/N}=\\{1\\}$ (Lemma \\ref{acap}), which is impossible because $a$ has order $p$ (Lemma \\ref{a,b}). Therefore the restriction of $i_a$ to $\\langle b\\rangle^{F/N}$ has order exactly $p$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"proof","item":"proof","head":"environment","scope":"paper","proves":"lem-8","attach":3,"span":{"file":"Fix-point-free.splitting.tex","from":174,"to":178},"hash":"sha256:a259833daf83274818e0e3d43d19e278a6a7b680638344cda446d4c061d96cf9","minhash":"","census":"2610.15360v1#proof-6","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"5OK9","kind":"statement","subkind":"lemma","paper":"2610.15360","version":"1","local":"lem-9","file":"","label":"ns","number":"5.2","section":"sec-5","order":16,"title":"Lemma","body_md":"\\label{ns}\n\t\tThe inner automorphism $i_a$ of the group ${F/N}$ is not a splitting automorphism of the subgroup $\\langle b\\rangle^{F/N}$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"lem","item":"statement/lemma","head":"environment","counter":"thm","number_from":"render","labels":["ns"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"here","origin":"own","span":{"file":"Fix-point-free.splitting.tex","from":180,"to":182},"hash":"sha256:08ac05e10e057fe579486649b41643730585a2f3aa1ec3217ebbfed2dc8f279b","minhash":"","census":"2610.15360v1#env-10","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"BUW9","kind":"proof","paper":"2610.15360","version":"1","local":"proof-7","file":"","section":"sec-5","order":17,"title":"Proof","body_md":"If $i_a$ were a splitting automorphism, then the identity\n\t\t\\[\n\t\tg\\,g^{i_a}g^{i_a^2}\\cdots g^{i_a^{p-1}}=1\n\t\t\\]\n\t\twould hold for every $g\\in \\langle b\\rangle^{F/N}$. In particular, for $g=b^{-1}$ we would obtain\n\t\t\\[\n\t\t{(b^{-1})}\\,{(b^{-1})}^{i_a}{(b^{-1})}^{i_a^2}\\cdots {(b^{-1})}^{i_a^{p-1}}=({b^{-1}}a^{-1})^pa^{p}=({b^{-1}}a^{-1})^p=1,\n\t\t\\]\n\t\twhich contradicts Lemma \\ref{ab}, according to which the element ${b^{-1}}a^{-1}=(ab)^{-1}$ has order $pq$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"proof","item":"proof","head":"environment","scope":"paper","proves":"lem-9","attach":3,"span":{"file":"Fix-point-free.splitting.tex","from":183,"to":193},"hash":"sha256:101cdf504fdcb6daf16b9273acff8a1f9453874450945f3a22cb55284b944070","minhash":"","census":"2610.15360v1#proof-7","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
